{'_client': <twikit.client.client.Client object at 0x000001E534E2AE40>, '_data': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1877197245420077412', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxODEwNDAwMTM4MTM0OTQ5ODg4', 'rest_id': '1810400138134949888', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': True, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Mon Jul 08 19:46:56 +0000 2024', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': "USNavy, VFW, Idaho native, retired\nBefore you follow, I'll likely say \nsomething you don't like at some point", 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 3695, 'followers_count': 1090, 'friends_count': 1266, 'has_custom_timelines': False, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 2, 'location': 'Northern Idaho', 'media_count': 118, 'name': 'Mark B', 'normal_followers_count': 1090, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': [], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1857982448967233537/Z77bHjly_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': '72Zorad', 'statuses_count': 2870, 'translator_type': 'none', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1877197245420077412'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736397274000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '7464', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzcxOTcyNDUyNjA2NDAyNTY=', 'text': "This was interesting so I did some math (the math gets a bit squishy as definitive numbers about CO2 per acre of burned forest are hard to quantify).\n\nWe're sitting on 60 acres of heavily wooded land in the pacific northwest, surround by thousands of acres of USFS and BLM land.\n\nWe had a forestry assessment done, and I looked it up.  Our 60 acres has 605 million board feet of lumber.  We'll use that even though what would burn in a forest fire is much much higher because board feet is the actual lumber value you get from the trees.  It doesn't include, waste, limbs, dead fall already down, small unharvestable trees, etc.\n\nIf our 60 acres burned it would create 422,000 metric tons of CO2.\n\nThe US produces 6.343 billion metric tons of CO2 annually.\n\nSo, if just our 60 acres burned it would create 1/15,030th the total US CO2 output a year.\n\nSeems small but consider this.  \n\n1) Burning the land owned by just 2 people, 1/168,000,000th of the population,  would contribute 1/15,030th of the total US CO2 emissions from all the people and companies in the US combined.    \n\n2) 60 acres is a square just over 1/4 mile on each side.  The US government owns 32,812 square miles of forest in Idaho.  That's 131,248 'parcels' the size of ours just in Idaho, and just the federal government.  There's also plenty of private forested land.\n\n3) To burn enough Idaho forest to equal US CO2 creation you would have to burn 2,404,800 acres or just under 1/8th of the 16.3 million acres of federal forest in Idaho (this doesn't include the vast swaths of private land that would also burn).\n\n4) Over the past 27 years an average of roughly 457,778 acres burn per year in Idaho.\n\n5) So every 5.2 years Idaho forest fires contribute as much CO2 as every person and company in the US produces in a year combined.\n\nThis is just Idaho fires.\nThink we should manage our forests?  Btw, we used to, and yes, even rakes where used.\n\nBut let's keep talking about reducing climate change from fossil fuels.", 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'lega